---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Linh Nguyen">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto pt-8 pb-16">
    <a href="/journals" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline"
      >&larr; Back to Journals</a
    >
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mt-8 mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      On Guitar
    </h1>
    <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 mb-8 italic">August 27, 2020</p>
    <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-4">
      <p class="text-lg">
        Lately, I've been playing guitar a lot more. Part of the motivation is
        practical: whenever I arrange music knowing I'll be the one recording
        the parts, I dumb down the difficulty of the arrangement (and it would
        certainly be nice to have enough performance proficiency that I didn't
        have these constraints). But mostly, I've been playing more because it
        brings me calm and joy in a way that no other hobby has during this
        strange time.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        When the topic of music comes up and people ask what I play, I rarely
        even mention guitar. Guitar is by far my most sketchily-acquired skill
        and the instrument I "understand" the least. Whenever I hear a tune or
        get a composition/arranging idea, working it out on guitar would be 10x
        slower than on piano.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        I attribute this mostly to the lack of strong foundation. In college,
        there was a guitar that lived in the common area of [a fraternity that
        my friends were in]. I didn't own a guitar outside of that, so I would
        just noodle around any time I stayed late at one of their parties. [Some
        friends] taught me the basics - I would mirror their hands, look up
        chords and tabs on my phone, and gradually piece things together.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        Anyone who seriously plays guitar knows that this is the wrong (albeit a
        common) way to learn. You're supposed build fretboard fluency, practice
        scales, and read music notation instead of relying on tabs. Predictably,
        by learning with haphazard methods and suboptimal technique, my progress
        has been far slower than it was for other instruments.
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg">
        Unpredictably, though, it's the instrument I've been gravitating towards
        the most lately. With all other instruments, I carry lots of baggage.
        There are specific goals I want to achieve and skills I want to develop.
        Any mistakes reinforce a critical, discouraging inner voice that makes
        it hard to maintain a consistent practice habit. I'm constantly
        relearning this same lesson: there's a delicate balance to strike
        between grinding at something vs. putting too much pressure on the
        outcome. What's most important is cultivating a mindset that brings you
        back to the activity - not out of obligation, but joy.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        I've been pretty goal-oriented my whole life, and I don't see something
        so core to who I am changing anytime soon (especially with music, thanks
        to the paralyzing perfectionism that comes from starting out in
        something like classical piano). But with guitar, I'm resigned to always
        being kind of mediocre and I have no grand ambitions for this hobby
        beyond having fun. I'm perfectly content playing the same songs over and
        over again, learning nothing new. It slows down my thoughts and brings
        me peace. Sometimes I wonder how fulfilled I would feel if I applied
        part of this mindset to work, exercise, or other creative hobbies.
      </p>
      <p class="text-lg">
        Anywho, in the spirit of embracing the joy for its own sake, I recorded
        something I've been practicing recently - mistakes and all.
      </p>
    </div>
    <video
      src="/videos/guitar-practice.mp4"
      controls
      class="mx-auto my-8 max-w-lg w-full rounded"
    >
      Your browser does not support the video tag.
    </video>
  </div>
</Layout>
